"The earthquake of the Day of Resurrection is a tremendous event. Describing the dread of that day, this surah informs us that some people dispute concerning Allah without having any knowledge and follow every rebellious devil. It has been decreed concerning that devil: Whoever takes him as an ally, he will surely mislead him and lead him to the punishment of Hell.\n\nO people! If you are in doubt about the resurrection after death, do not doubt. For We created you from dust, then from a drop of fluid (nutfah), then from a clinging clot (alaqah), then from a lump of flesh—formed and unformed. We cause whom We will to remain in the wombs for an appointed term, then We bring you forth as infants, and then [foster you] that you may reach your prime of maturity. Some of you die, and some of you are brought back to the feeblest old age, so that after having known something they know nothing at all. And you see the earth barren and dry; but when We send down water upon it, it stirs, swells, and brings forth every kind of delightful pair of plants. These demonstrate that Allah is the Truth, that He gives life to the dead, and that He is capable of all things. The Hour is surely coming, and Allah will resurrect everyone in the graves.\n\nAnd among people is he who disputes concerning Allah without knowledge, without guidance, and without an illuminating book, turning away in arrogance to mislead others from the path of Allah. For him in this world is disgrace, and on the Day of Resurrection We will make him taste the punishment of the Burning Fire. It is said: 'These are on account of the sins your hands have earned.' Undoubtedly, Allah is never unjust to His servants.\n\nAnd among people is he who worships Allah as if on the edge of a cliff: if good befalls him, his heart is at ease with it; but if a trial strikes him, he turns around on his face. He loses both this world and the Hereafter. That is the manifest loss. Instead of Allah, he calls upon things that can neither harm him nor benefit him. That is the straying far astray. He calls upon one whose harm is closer than his benefit; what an evil protector and what an evil companion!\n\nThere is no doubt that Allah will admit those who believe and do righteous deeds into gardens beneath which rivers flow. Allah does whatever He wills. Whoever thinks that Allah will not help the Prophet in this world and the Hereafter, let him extend a rope to the ceiling, then strangle himself and see whether his scheme will remove what enrages him. Thus have We sent down the Quran as clear verses. Allah guides whom He wills to the right path.\n\nIndeed, those who believe, the Jews, the Sabians, the Christians, the Magians (fire-worshippers), and those who associate partners with Allah—Allah will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection. For Allah is a witness over all things. Have you not seen that whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on the earth, the sun, the moon, the stars, the mountains, the trees, the beasts, and many among mankind prostrate to Allah? And upon many the punishment has become due. And whomever Allah humiliates and demeans, none can honor him. Allah does whatever He wills.\n\nThese two are two adversaries who dispute concerning their Lord: for those who disbelieve, garments of fire have been cut out, and boiling water will be poured over their heads, melting whatever is in their bellies as well as their skins. For them are maces of iron. Whenever they try to get out from there on account of anguish, they are driven back: 'Taste the burning punishment!' Allah will admit those who believe and do good deeds into gardens beneath which rivers flow; they will be adorned therein with bracelets of gold and pearls, and their garments will be of silk. They have both been brought to a state where they hear the best of speech and guided to the path of Allah, the Praiseworthy.\n\nThose who disbelieve and bar others from the path of Allah and from the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram)—which has been made equal for both the resident and the traveler—whoever intends therein deviation through wrongdoing, We will make him taste a painful punishment. And remember when We prepared the site of the Ka'bah for Abraham: 'Do not associate anything with Me, and purify My House for those who perform tawaf, those who stand in prayer, and those who bow and prostrate.' Announce to mankind the Hajj; they will come to you on foot and on every lean mount, crossing every deep valley, so that they may witness benefits for themselves and mention the name of Allah on appointed days over the cattle He has provided for them as sustenance. Eat from them yourselves, and feed the destitute. Then let them end their untidiness, fulfill their vows, and perform tawaf around the Ka'bah.\n\nThis is the command. Whoever honors the sacred ordinances of Allah, that is good for him in the sight of his Lord. All animals have been made lawful to you, except what has been announced to you. So avoid the filth of idols and avoid false speech. Be people who associate no partners with Allah and believe in His oneness. Whoever associates partners with Allah is like something that has fallen from the sky and birds have snatched it away, or the wind has swept it into an abyss. Whoever honors the symbols of Allah (the sacrificial animals), that is undoubtedly from the piety of hearts. For you in them are benefits until an appointed term; then their final destination is at the Ancient House (Bayt al-'Atiq, the Ka'bah).\n\nFor every community We have appointed a place of worship so that they may mention the name of Allah over the sacrificial animals He has provided for them as sustenance. Your God is One God; so submit to Him. Give glad tidings to the humble: those whose hearts tremble when Allah is mentioned, who are patient over what befalls them, who perform prayer, and who spend in the way of Allah out of the sustenance We have given them. We have made the sacrificial camels and cattle among the symbols of Allah for you; in them is good for you. Mention the name of Allah over them as they are slaughtered, and when their flanks rest on the ground, eat from them and feed both the content who do not ask and the needy who ask. Thus has He subjected them to you so that you may be grateful. Their meat and their blood do not reach Allah; rather, what reaches Him is your piety. He has subjected them to you in this manner so that you may magnify Allah. Give glad tidings to the doers of good.\n\nThere are various interpretations regarding the expression Bayt al-'Atiq. First, the word 'atiq, like 'itaq, means brand-new and precious, which indicates that the Ka'bah is an honorable and venerable house. According to the second view, 'atiq means emancipated and free; the Ka'bah was given this name because it has been protected from the attacks of tyrannical despots. Indeed, it has been reported in a hadith that Allah protects the Ka'bah from the malice of tyrants. The fate of aggressors such as Tubba' and Abrahah serves as an example of this. A third interpretation states that 'atiq means 'fundamentally free' because it is the property of no one. Since it is impossible to express all these meanings in a single word, the term 'Bayt al-'Atiq' should be preserved as it is.\n\nHonoring the things made sacred by Allah is good for a person. Eat from the livestock (an'am) that have been made lawful to you; however, carrion, blood, and animals slaughtered in the name of anyone other than Allah are forbidden. Beware of idols and false speech; reject pre-Islamic pagan customs of the Jahiliyyah such as Bahirah, Sa'ibah, Wasiylah, and Ham. Avoid associating partners with Allah; shirk (associating partners with Allah) destroys a person just like an object falling from the sky that birds snatch away or that the wind scatters.\n\nHonoring the symbols of Allah is from the piety of hearts. The sacrificial animals slaughtered during Hajj provide benefits for you until an appointed term, and then they reach the Ancient House (Bayt al-'Atiq, the Ka'bah). For every community We have appointed a place of sacrifice so that they may mention the name of Allah. Therefore, submit to Allah, the One God. Give glad tidings to the humble whose hearts tremble when Allah is mentioned, who are patient over what befalls them, perform prayer, and spend out of what they have been provided.\n\nThe badana (large sacrificial animals) are among the symbols of Allah; mention the name of Allah over them as they are slaughtered. Camels are slaughtered standing while tied, and small livestock are slaughtered lying down. Once they have fallen lifeless, eat from them, and feed both the contented one and the beggar. Allah has subjected them to you so that you may give thanks. Neither their meat nor their blood reaches Allah; rather, it is only your piety (taqwa) that reaches Him. Thus has He subjected them to you so that you may glorify Allah for having guided you.\n\nAllah defends those who believe; He does not love any treacherous, ungrateful person. Permission [to fight] has been given to the oppressed against whom war is waged; they were expelled from their homes merely for saying, 'Our Lord is Allah.' Had Allah not repelled some people by means of others, monasteries, churches, synagogues, and mosques would have been demolished. Allah surely helps those who help His cause. Those believers are such that when given authority in the land, they establish the prayer, give zakah, enjoin what is right, and forbid what is wrong. And to Allah belongs the outcome of all matters.\n\nIn verses 67–70, it is emphasized that a sacred law (sharia—a system of worship and legal order) has been prescribed for every community, and that they should not dispute with the Prophet (peace be upon him) regarding this matter. It is stated that inviting people to their Lord is upon true guidance. If they dispute, they should be told that Allah knows best what they do, and it is reminded that their disagreements will be resolved by Allah on the Day of Resurrection. It is expressed that Allah knows all that is in the heavens and the earth, and that all of this is recorded in a Book (the Preserved Tablet / al-Lawh al-Mahfuz).\n\nIn verses 71–72, it is related that the polytheists worship, besides Allah, things for which He has revealed no authority and about which they have no knowledge, and that the wrongdoers have no helper. The denial of the disbelievers is recognizable upon their faces; they are on the verge of attacking those who recite the verses to them. They are informed of the Fire, which is even worse than this, and it is stated that it is an evil destination for the disbelievers.\n\nIn verses 73–74, a parable is presented: the things worshipped besides Allah cannot create even a single fly; and if a fly were to snatch something away from them, they could not rescue it. Both the seeker and the sought are helpless. They have not appraised Allah with the appraisal due to Him; whereas Allah is indeed All-Powerful, Almighty.\n\nVerses 75–76 state that Allah chooses messengers from the angels and from mankind, and that He is All-Hearing, All-Seeing. He knows what is before them and what is behind them, and all matters are returned to Allah.\n\nIn verse 77, the believers are commanded to bow down (ruku), prostrate (sujud), (that is, perform the prayer), worship their Lord, and do good. According to a narration, until this verse was revealed, bowing and prostrating were performed separately in prayer; with this verse, it was commanded that the two be united within a single prayer. The prostration mentioned here is not a prostration of recitation (tilawah), but rather one of the essential pillars of prayer. Imam al-Shafi'i held that a prostration of recitation is required at this verse. The command to 'do good' encompasses voluntary acts of worship alongside obligatory duties, maintaining ties of kinship, displaying noble character, and benefiting others. Salvation can only be hoped for through the grace of Allah.\n\nVerse 78 commands striving (jihad) in the cause of Allah with the striving due to Him. Jihad is of three kinds: striving against the open enemy, against Satan, and against the lower self (nafs). Most correctly, it encompasses all three. Allah has chosen this community and has not placed any hardship upon you in religion; He has granted ease. As was the case in the religion of your father Abraham, He named you 'Muslims' both previously and in the Quran, so that the Messenger may be a witness over you and you may be witnesses over mankind. So establish the prayer, give zakah, and hold fast to Allah. He is your Protector and your Helper."